erms they struck down  over 200 federal state and local  economic regulations to protect the  sacred right of powerful corporations  the court also took huge steps to limit  federal power to protect the most  vulnerable for example they decided that  a federal ban on interstate shipment of  goods produced by child labor was  unconstitutional because that should be  left up to the states at the time states  that wanted to ban child labor risked  losing their competitive business  advantages to states that engaged in it  economic pressure those kept child labor  alive and well and the country saw  millions of children working in deadly  conditions for decades now they just  have to go to school for deadly  conditions we're not going to put that  in the video but it's and it's dark and  it's true so you know what i dare you  editor put it in there speak truth to  power note that just a decade earlier  the court had ruled that interstate  shipment of lottery tickets could be  banned by the federal government but hey  protecting the nation from out-of-state  lottery tickets but not from child labor  seems totally normal and reasonable  again what we're seeing in this time  period is a general reluctance to  protect vulnerable people from  government or corporate power it's easy  now to look back and lament the way the  supreme court functioned pre-civil war  or in the gilded age but it's worth more  closely examining what happened under  the court in its more progressive eras  of history take chief justice earl  warren's court often considered the last  one to truly represent the interests of  the common man earlier courts weren't  known for expanding civil rights and  liberties but warren was openly  committed to social justice what's more  he was likable and persuasive which led  to even some of the most controversial  cases that came across scotus's desk  reaching unanimous or near unanimous  decisions from the justices which is  basically unheard of today for such  divisive cases brown v board of  education the landmark decision which  reversed the separate but equal doctrine  was decided unanimously this is directly  because of warren's influence according  to another justice's autobiography had  the supreme court ruled in 1952 when the  case was initially argued and before  warren joined the court  there would have been a 5-4 vote to  uphold segregation what in the actual  [ __ ]  what in the actual  then there was gideon v wainwright which  found that everyone has the right to  legal counsel in a criminal case and  loving v virginia which ruled that laws  banning interracial marriage were  unconstitutional these decisions helped  create a constitutional precedent that  allowed congress to pass other important  pieces of legislation like the civil  rights act of 1964 and the voting rights  act of 1965 but not everyone was happy  with this progress especially the  definitely not a crook richard nixon  nixon maintained a 30-year beef with  earl warren who would refuse to endorse  him earlier in his political career so  when nixon became president he wanted to  tear down everything that warren had  built and due to some squabbling that  foreshadowed barack obama's nomination  of merrick garland being squashed nixon  was able to fill four seats over the  course of his short presidency his goal  he said was to appoint  strict constructionists these guys would  be interpreting the law not making the  law they would see themselves as  caretakers of the constitution and  servants of the people not super  legislators with a free hand to impose  their political and social viewpoints at  first glance this sounds relatively  apolitical but it was actually the first  glimmer of an important philosophy that  would change the way the supreme court  functioned and nixon's strict  constructionist judges were expected to  roll back some of the warren court's  broad constructionist decisions to  expand human rights that's why things  like abortion or same-sex marriage  aren't ruled expressly legal by the  supreme court but instead use  constitutional reasoning like the equal  protection clause and the right to  privacy this is also what makes any  rights not expressly delineated in the  constitution so tenuous even if they're  popular with citizens and while nixon's  appointee chief justice burger did not  roll back the warren court's decisions  on mass as expected his appointment  cemented an ideological shift in the  court and went from expanding civil  liberties to seeing such expansions of  rights as outside of the purview of the  constitution like its earlier iterations  this new court regularly ruled in favor  of corporate and institutional interest  against regular citizens today the  supreme court has several justices who  follow strict constructionism  specifically what is called textualism  which holds that the constitution should  be interpreted the way your average  person in 1787 would have interpreted it  how does that account for say the rise  of global capitalism or the invention of  the ak-47 or the rise of the internet  you might be wondering it doesn't really  but surely you must be wondering what to  make of those few exciting supreme court  cases you learned about in high school  which takes us back to the warren court  because it evokes a more romantic vision  of the institution and yet even some of  its most groundbreaking decisions look  kind of mealy-mouthed in retrospect take  brown v board which ended legal  government-mandated segregation surely  we're not going to critique this ruling  right well  in reality while the warren court struck  down lots of individual cases brought to  them about segregation in courtrooms on  buses on golf courses and restaurants  and so on they never wrote an opinion  that said state governments actually had  to do anything to actively end  segregation or prevent it from happening  quite the opposite in fact as robert l  carter wrote in the michigan law review  in 1968 the court ruled that a state is  under no affirmative obligation to enact  anti-discrimination legislation and that  such laws may be repealed but the court  added the precautionary caveat that if  in the process of repeal the state tips  the political balance in favor of racial  discrimination it violates the 14th  amendment so a state not only didn't  have to put any laws in place to end  discrimination but they also were within  their rights to repeal  anti-discrimination legislation as long  as they didn't do an oopsie and tip the  scales towards intentional  discrimination which is totally a very  easy and objective thing to measure and  prevent thus i'm sure you'll be shocked  to hear that virtually no school  desegregation actually happened for  years after brown it wasn't until the  civil rights act of 1964 that any  meaningful progress was made in  desegregation and that was limited  mostly to cities in the south in san  antonio independent school district v  rodriguez scotus even went so far as to  rule that inequalities in school funding  aren't illegal because get this the  right to equal education  isn't in the constitution that's right  scotus ruled that it's fine for schools  to be both separate and unequal as long  as the segregation isn't mandated by the  government and in the 50 years since  brown the supreme court has made america  segregation and inequality problems even  worse like the 1991 decision in which  the oklahoma city school board ceased  busing black students to white schools  as a desegregation effort because  schools had become less segregated  unsurprisingly when the busing ended the  schools became segregated again and  while there's plenty wrong with buzzing  scotus ruled schools don't have to be  equal in terms of funding and you can  probably guess whose schools weren't  being funded still the court ruled that  federal acts like bussing were only  meant as a temporary measure to remedy  past discrimination 